#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064117} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3aaba0a-3b86-58c3-bbc8-22ced09bc8a8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Definite Integral Using Integration by Parts"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065444 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}{v}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & =\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}x}\\cdot {\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}}_{{\\color{blue}u}{\\color{red}v}}-\\int \\underbrace{{\\color{red}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}1}}_{{\\color{red}v}{\\color{blue}{u}^{'}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ and replace it with the simpler $$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ plus some additional terms." :tutorial/id #uuid "7ef18e8c-9fd4-5e14-84ee-a8fe309425c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 892 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065445 :tutorial/content "If you thought the by-parts formula looked similar to the product rule for differentiation, then you'd be right. In fact, that's precisely where it comes from!\n\nTo work out the formula, we recall the product rule for differentiation,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)=u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}+ v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x},\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ and $v$ are functions of $x.$ \n\nIf we rearrange this formula a bit, we get\n\n$$\nu\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)- v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now integrate both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)\\,\\textrm{d}x- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the fundamental theorem of calculus, we can use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)\\,\\textrm{d}x=uv,\n$$ and we arrive at the by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9f435fc9-cb87-577b-89e1-cda2ce506387" :tutorial/math-academy-id 895 :tutorial/title "Deriving the By Parts Formula From the Product Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065446 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our first example,\n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that when we selected our $u$ and $v',$ we could have picked them the other way around. So instead, we could have said\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the by-parts formula using this selection, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, while the above is a mathematically correct statement, the integral on the right-hand side is *more* complicated than the integral we started with. Ouch!\n\nTherefore, it's crucial that we select our $u$ and $v'$ in such a way that the problem is simplified, not made more complicated." :tutorial/id #uuid "e7845bcc-bef4-5a31-8abe-39845317fbaf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 896 :tutorial/title "Final Remark"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087515} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88c1deb-cd11-5b68-b90b-2d8edfae2469" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087516} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de557d1d-5f27-50e3-bcba-22b21f83267f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087517} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088902 :tutorial/content "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $\\color{red}a$ and a vertical radius of length $\\color{blue}b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{{\\color{red}a}^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{{\\color{blue}b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/92/9227839bbf9a01312c3014d336a88176eecd73aa934fcd4741c11271b1e0bea6.png)\n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the equation of the following ellipse. The horizontal radius has length $\\color{red}a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $\\color{blue}b=2.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe7dff90438e9571ae599a446e355776fbd36ed4746dd76e6633f3848e1f6bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{{\\color{red}3}}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{{\\color{blue}2}}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dfd31765-db4b-5a8f-a4f9-3cd31b487aea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3347 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088903 :tutorial/content "The **axes** of an ellipse are the horizontal and vertical diameters of the ellipse.\n\n- The **major axis** of an ellipse is the *larger* of the horizontal and vertical diameters.\n\n- The **minor axis** of an ellipse is the *shorter* of the horizontal and vertical diameters.\n\nFor example, the ellipse below has a major axis of $6$ and a minor axis of $4.$\n\n![](images/42/426ef9412a96730e50d78437fce2f1d45d7be4d4c175e3a230f4a696e0811189.png)\n\nThe **semi-axes** of an ellipse are the horizontal and vertical radii of the ellipse.\n\n- The **semi-major axis** of an ellipse is the *larger* of the horizontal and vertical radii. In other words, it is half the length of the major axis.\n\n- The **semi-minor axis** of an ellipse is the *shorter* of the horizontal and vertical radii. In other words, it is half the length of the minor axis.\n\nFor example, the ellipse below has a semi-major axis of length $3$ and a semi-minor axis of length $2.$\n\n![](images/b0/b031900464b03a9c2c2a7a9fe4cfaf40932f8d0c76b9d952cebf64d82a6261bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fd403f68-d6b8-5336-b699-87bcfaeadef8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3348 :tutorial/title "Major and Minor Axes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096447} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a66a99-6b4a-5ad5-8922-6f48006ff183" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096493} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b11642dd-80cc-539c-8c2d-8cdee58e9d3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096448} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44ac67b-5e3d-5b1e-bf2c-554b592ba29e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"}]}
